#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:5
#: d27c92c8936e46748347c132df57f6c2
msgid "Tuning Ground Steering for a Plane"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:7
#: f8afe6de4a2b48c4bf8ecc9b3c148164
msgid "The Plane firmware has the ability to control ground steering if you have either a tail wheel or nose wheel in your aircraft. Ground steering is activated in all modes except MANUAL, and when properly tuned it makes it much easier to keep a plane on a straight track along the runway for takeoff and landing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:13
#: a5d20d2b35a14ea6af9168375a1e25b8
msgid "Ground steering may be implemented either by using a wheel coupled to a RUDDER output (``SERVOx_FUNCTION`` =21), either mechanically or with an second servo, or with the dedicated GroundSteering output (``SERVOx_FUNCTION`` =26). If the GroundSteering output is assigned, then it operates independently of the RUDDER output, otherwise, the Ground Steering controller's output controls the RUDDER when below :ref:`GROUND_STEER_ALT<GROUND_STEER_ALT>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:15
#: 8a58952aa6d44b3e965d3e8f6867a0b4
msgid "When setting up ground steering for a plane there are 4 key parameters you need to get right. This guide provides a set of steps to get those parameters right, so that your plane will accurately track down a runway on the ground."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:21
#: 3fb358de3ab44b86bbac7f30e00da33b
msgid "Controlling steering"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:23
#: d6c50860515642babc939cb00c134344
msgid "To enable ground steering you need to set the :ref:`GROUND_STEER_ALT<GROUND_STEER_ALT>` parameter to something above zero. This is the barometric altitude above the initial altitude at which the steering changes from ground steering of a wheel to rudder steering for aerodynamic control. It is recommended that you set this to 5 meters to allow for a bit of barometric drift."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:29
#: 7577d67b84044efe82ad5f6bb6db926c
msgid "When ground steering is active it locks onto a compass heading whenever you have no rudder or aileron input. So to steer the plane on the ground while taxiing you should use the rudder to change direction, then take your hand off both rudder and aileron to hold the current compass heading. Ground steering will only work properly if you have a compass enabled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:37
#: 80ba420dd73a431480942737fd6e3d72
msgid "THE KEY PARAMETERS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:39
#: dcb4b0e5f92648138a696470ed7ad0be
msgid ":ref:`STEER2SRV_P<STEER2SRV_P>`: This tells the code what the turning circle (as a diameter in meters) is for your plane. It is critical that you get this parameter right, as it tells the code what steering angle to choose to achieve a desired turn rate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:43
#: 2f4fd002f2cd4540b0e3e42e7bc5bab4
msgid ":ref:`STEER2SRV_I<STEER2SRV_I>`: This is the gain from the integral of steering angle. Increasing this gain causes the controller to trim out steady offsets due to an out of trim vehicle.."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:46
#: c6f36a6261294d6fb150e045efe574d0
msgid ":ref:`STEER2SRV_D<STEER2SRV_D>`: This adjusts the damping of the steering control loop. This gain helps to reduce steering jitter with vibration. It should be increased in 0.01 increments as too high a value can lead to a high frequency steering oscillation that could overstress the vehicle."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:51
#: f96337cb45b14b8aac736d0397deaab8
msgid ":ref:`STEER2SRV_IMAX<STEER2SRV_IMAX>`: This limits the number of degrees of steering in centi-degrees over which the integrator will operate. At the default setting of 1500 centi-degrees, the integrator will be limited to +- 15 degrees of servo travel. The maximum servo deflection is +- 45 centi-degrees, so the default value represents a 1/3rd of the total control throw which is adequate unless the vehicle is severely out of trim."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:58
#: 1c5bd938be8b43b28085459c8c9348ff
msgid ":ref:`STEER2SRV_MINSPD<STEER2SRV_MINSPD>`: This is the minimum assumed ground speed in meters/second for steering. Having a minimum speed prevents oscillations when the vehicle first starts moving. The vehicle can still driver slower than this limit, but the steering calculations will be done based on this minimum speed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:63
#: cb2495c21e9c453bbef05c04052bd698
msgid ":ref:`STEER2SRV_TCONST<STEER2SRV_TCONST>`: This controls the time constant in seconds from demanded to achieved steering angle. A value of 0.75 is a good default and will work with nearly all rovers. Ground steering in aircraft needs a bit smaller time constant, and a value of 0.5 is recommended for best ground handling in fixed wing aircraft. A value of 0.75 means that the controller will try to correct any deviation between the desired and actual steering angle in 0.75 seconds. Advanced users may want to reduce this time to obtain a faster response but there is no point setting a time less than the vehicle can achieve."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:73
#: e83ab65bb3bd48c389026ea5b199c83a
msgid ":ref:`GROUND_STEER_ALT<GROUND_STEER_ALT>`: Altitude at which to use the ground steering controller on the rudder. If non-zero, then the STEER2SRV controller will be used to control the rudder for altitudes below this limit with respect to the home altitude."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:79
#: b3e631d4058d4b0b82c325fe4cc6dfd8
msgid "STEP 1: SETTING INITIAL PARAMETERS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:81
#: 902dac59738f458b9ace0d0fdf75a8ab
msgid "To start the tuning process set the following values:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:83
#: c75867a7316c447cb406fd84ea92e937
msgid "**STEER2SRV_P**:             =      See step 2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:84
#: 86898e36cdad4bff8f8a3de22a817643
msgid "**STEER2SRV_I**:              =      0.1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:85
#: 58e0c006484a4e8ab7868ab623d65fa7
msgid "**STEER2SRV_D**:             =     0.02"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:86
#: 1af38ec7a5f244329e80dc9e0c1d64a5
msgid "**STEER2SRV_IMAX**:      =    1500"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:87
#: 703e922da0d841739b6807144a2668f3
msgid "**STEER2SRV_MINSPD**: =    1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:88
#: eb729b60d091492e9554cda0317558f0
msgid "**STEER2SRV_TCONST**: =    0.5"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:89
#: c755f4ab4b0444f5ba463dc26a51002a
msgid "**GROUND_STEER_ALT**: =    5"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:91
#: 362943ff17994c5885936cadb17169e4
msgid "These are conservative values that should give you reasonable behaviour for most PLANES."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:95
#: 2909c6cdf1fa43f5a49b33bd33ebe33f
msgid "STEP 2: SETTING THE :ref:`STEER2SRV_P<STEER2SRV_P>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:97
#: c7596746a98141e0b47a98b9990de9dd
msgid "To set your :ref:`STEER2SRV_P<STEER2SRV_P>` parameter you need to measure the diameter of the turning circle of your plane."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:100
#: 59fb21785df74ffcaa5572730778561a
msgid "With your motor off put your plane into MANUAL mode, and put the rudder hard over to one side. Then slowly push your plane in a circle. Use a tape measure to measure the diameter of that circle and set :ref:`STEER2SRV_P<STEER2SRV_P>` to that value in meters. Most planes have a turning diameter of around 4 meters. If your turning diameter is a long way from 4 then you may have the rates or throw on your rudder set incorrectly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:108
#: 9a84dafc205d4b1da8fb055eaa4f5a6d
msgid "Fixing Problems with weaving"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:110
#: 17706d947b64409eac68844add5ae812
msgid "A common issue with ground steering is that the steering 'weaves', turning from side to side rather than turning smoothly. There can be several possible reasons for this happening."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:114
#: 46b2437118ae43749a1a8ce4fd16ebe8
msgid "The first thing you need to work out is if the problem is confined to low speed or also affects higher speed driving. If the problem only happens at very low speed then the most likely problem is that :ref:`STEER2SRV_MINSPD<STEER2SRV_MINSPD>` is too low. The default is 1.0 m/s, which is quite low, and if your GPS heading isn't very reliable at low speed then you may need to raise that number. Try 2.0 and see if that helps with low speed weaving."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:122
#: 38ffaa2d498d475ca4d6d2b6a5da1504
msgid "In the steering controller there are 3 key parameters that will control weaving:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:125
#: 17f37039ef5c41b79acf31bd992cfb26
msgid "A smaller :ref:`STEER2SRV_P<STEER2SRV_P>` will reduce weaving, try reducing it by 0.1 at a time"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:127
#: e95501d5c67748f7afe4893440ebe08c
msgid "A larger :ref:`STEER2SRV_D<STEER2SRV_D>` will \"damp\" the weaving, but if you make it too large then you will get high speed oscillation. For example you may find that a value of 0.1 reduces the damping, but a value of 0.2 could cause a high speed oscillation in the steering servo. If you get fast oscillation then reduce the :ref:`STEER2SRV_D<STEER2SRV_D>` value by 50%."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:132
#: d1fcbfaaddf44f4ab746635361a137d3
msgid "A larger :ref:`STEER2SRV_TCONST<STEER2SRV_TCONST>` will slow down the steering controller, which will reduce weaving. Try raising it in steps of 0.1."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:135
#: 842c208a504242e4aeba555c64533ed9
msgid "With the current controller system you do need to experiment a bit with these values to get the behaviour you want."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:139
#: 332205334eda40de90059975e0a30dae
msgid "Tuning for auto-takeoff"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:141
#: bbb5a25bc6d242e69d09929c423ded44
msgid "To tune the ground steering for auto-takeoff it is generally best to do the tuning in FBWA mode, and only change to auto-takeoff once you can takeoff in FBWA mode without overriding the steering at all (ie. takeoff without using the rudder stick)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:146
#: 5f85d3fbee714957b35389135d2b7fba
msgid "It can also be very helpful to use the :ref:`FBWA Takeoff Elevator <takeoff-fbwa>` option to setup the same automated elevator behaviour as is used in auto-takeoff when in FBWA mode. That helps keep tail wheels on the ground in taildragger aircraft (and can also be used to keep nose wheels on the ground in tricycle aircraft)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:153
#: 6fbf53a833e14765a3b7fd3c7a53e829
msgid "Limiting the steering to prevent a roll on a wing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:155
#: 1cadc63bfc7d488fae5d75c1bc4ad72b
msgid "On a large fast planes, especially with large wingspan, there is a chance on rolling on the wing if the steering is too aggressive. To coupe with this problem steering limiting can be setup that will derate steering angles with increasing speed. When the speed is lower than the set derating speed, no limit occurs and the angle for steering is passed through without changes. When the speed increase, the steering angle starts to decrease at a setup rate until the minimum steering angle is reached."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:164
#: 06a9a9a8da494ca784bcfdd4a7554da4
msgid ":ref:`STEER2SRV_DRTSPD<STEER2SRV_DRTSPD>`: After this speed the steering angle gets reduced. The units are m/s"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:165
#: 82dfed03501c4cc1b13ecb41b8df554a
msgid ":ref:`STEER2SRV_DRTFCT<STEER2SRV_DRTFCT>`: Amount of degrees to reduce per each additional m/s increase in speed. The units are in °/m/s"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tuning-ground-steering-for-a-plane.rst:166
#: 6904caeb91ff48e3858315c2cd2ea14b
msgid ":ref:`STEER2SRV_DRTMIN<STEER2SRV_DRTMIN>`: Minimum angle in centi-degrees of steering that will not be decreased further"
msgstr ""

